Durarara!! AU
Chapter 18 – Part 3
They left the flat separately, like people returning borrowed versions of themselves to the day.
Curtis locked the door behind him, long black braids neat against the black compression shirt, the residual warmth of the hug still sitting under his skin as he stepped into the canceled-school quiet of Ikebukuro. Celty put the yellow helmet back on in the stairwell, black smoke sealed away from public view, and rolled Shooter out toward the life that expected her home soon.
Neither looked back long enough to make a scene of it.

Anri was waiting near a small café a few blocks from campus, hands folded around a cup she had ordered mostly for something to hold. When she saw Curtis, the tension in her shoulders eased by a visible degree.
“Curtis-kun.” A soft breath. “Thank you for coming.”
“Yeah.” He sat across from her, mismatched eyes half-lidded, present enough to be kind and distant enough that the morning still occupied a locked room behind them. “You all right?”
Anri nodded, then shook her head a little, then settled somewhere in between.
“I keep thinking about everything. The city. The people who disappear into it. The way some of us only find out too late that we needed someone standing closer.” Her fingers tightened around the cup. “When classes were canceled, I… didn’t want to sit with that alone.”
Curtis listened.
He was good at that. Better than he wanted to be. He asked small questions, let her talk, gave her the steady attention that made people feel less like they were drowning in their own heads. From the outside, it would have looked simple: a quiet transfer student and a careful girl sharing an unscheduled morning.
From the inside, every gentle response cost him a fraction more than it should have. Anri’s trust sat in the same city as Rio’s soft claim and Celty’s open door, and the weight of that geometry pressed under his ribs while he kept his face even.
Anri watched him over the rim of her cup, dark eyes thoughtful.
“…You look tired.”
Curtis gave a slight shrug, long black braids shifting.
“Didn’t sleep much.”
She accepted the partial truth without pushing, and for a while that was enough: shared table, shared quiet, a fragile ordinary hour neither of them named as more than it was.

Celty’s key turned in the apartment lock with the same practiced motion as always.
Shinra looked up from the table at once, bright as a man receiving confirmation that the system still worked.
“Celty! Welcome back. You were out all night after all. I was beginning to assemble theories.”
Mei sat a few feet away with a folder of notes, pink hair neat, expression mildly interested and not even slightly guilty. The air in the room still carried more than paper and tea, no matter what Shinra believed about Celty’s senses.
Celty set the yellow helmet down, black smoke rising briefly from the empty space above her collar before she sealed the private reaction shut.
Celty:
I was driving.
Shinra accepted it with a smile that made the lie feel almost kind of him to believe.
“Of course. The city gets into your blood that way. Mei and I were just reviewing some old case notes. Nothing dramatic.” He gestured toward the other woman with easy familiarity. “You remember Mei.”
Celty remembered.
She also remembered a roof, a hand in hers, a man who had looked at her without flinching and then opened a door on purpose. Standing in her own home, with Shinra’s devotion and Mei’s residual presence occupying the same air, the apartment felt newly misaligned — not false in every piece, but no longer continuous with the person who had left it the night before.
Mei offered a polite, unreadable smile.
“Good to see you again, Celty.”
Celty inclined the helmet slightly in answer, already typing something neutral, already performing the correct version of return. Inside, under the sealed channel no one else could hear, one thought repeated with quiet, dangerous clarity:
I could have stayed.
Across Ikebukuro, Curtis sat with Anri and did not say Celty’s name.
In the apartment, Celty stood with Shinra and Mei and did not say Curtis’s.
The day held.
The complication did not dissolve.
And both of them, in separate rooms of the same city, began the harder work of living as if the morning had not already changed the map.
Across the small café table, Curtis only half-heard the ambient clink of cups and low conversation around them.
The rest of him was stuck on the geometry under his ribs.
Rio, who had known him since the rooftop and the morning after the braids came down.
Anri, sitting right in front of him with careful eyes and quiet trust.
Celty, who had forced her way under his skin overnight in a manner no sealed helmet or practical boundary had managed to prevent — and whose open door still sat in his chest like a second pulse while Anri’s softer presence occupied the same hour.
Curtis’s mismatched eyes lowered briefly to the table.
…Oh dear.
The thought was almost dry. Almost helpless. He had come to Japan with a list and a dead brother’s unfinished future, not a growing constellation of people who each held a different true piece of him. And yet here he was, in a fitted black compression shirt he had never intended as outdoor company clothes, trying to be present for one girl while the morning’s other gravity still clung to his hands.
He looked up and offered Anri a small, apologetic smile, long black braids shifting against his shoulders.
“Sorry I didn’t bother dressing today. I normally do, but I had no time to get ready… and it would be rude of me to make you wait.”
Anri’s eyes flicked, almost involuntarily, over the close black fabric and the frame underneath it — the clear lines of muscle, the broad shoulders, the lived-in strength the compression shirt did nothing to hide. Color rose under her skin at once. She looked down at her cup, then back up again with a shy, startled honesty she could not quite swallow in time.
“…I don’t mind.”
A short pause. Her fingers tightened around the cup.
“The look, I mean. You don’t have to apologize for it.” Another quieter beat, softer and a little embarrassed by her own directness. “You still came. That matters more than whether you had time to change.”
She glanced at him once more, then away, the blush lingering as if her body had filed the image whether her manners approved or not.
At the apartment, the performance of a normal return lasted less than an hour.
Shinra talked. Mei contributed the occasional dry observation. Celty sat with her helmet on the table and her PDA in hand, offering short typed replies in the right places, occupying the correct chair like a woman who had merely been out driving all night and come home to familiar air. On the surface it worked. Shinra’s brightness filled the gaps. Mei’s presence remained carefully unsurprised. The room accepted the arrangement because it always had.
Under the surface, Celty felt the misalignment in every quiet second.
The couch was not the one she had sat on that morning.
The devotion in Shinra’s voice did not land in the same place it used to.
Mei’s easy occupancy of the space made the air feel already claimed by a version of intimacy Celty was no longer certain she understood, or wanted explained.
She was still deciding how long she could keep sitting inside that discrepancy when the courier channel lit up.
Multiple requests.
Time-sensitive.
Black Rider only.
Celty rose at once.
Celty:
Work.
Shinra looked up, immediately accommodating.
“Already? You only just came back. Still, the city never schedules around comfort, does it?” He smiled, fond and unbothered. “Be careful. I’ll keep the place running.”
Mei watched her over the notes, pink hair tipping slightly, expression unreadable.
“Duty calls.”
Celty took the yellow helmet, sealed herself back into the public version of the legend, and left the apartment with the same efficiency she always had. Shooter answered in the street below. The engine note cut cleanly through the daytime noise as she pulled out into Ikebukuro again — not fleeing in any way she intended to name, but grateful, privately, that the city had given her a legitimate reason not to sit much longer inside a home that no longer fit the shape of the morning she had just lived.
Across town, Curtis sat with Anri and tried not to look like a man being claimed from three directions at once.
On the road, Celty leaned into the next job and told herself motion was simpler than the apartment behind her.
Neither of them was wrong.
Neither of them was free of the complication either.
